.TH "TTBARS" "1" "July 2026" "ttbars" "User Commands"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
ttbars - minimalist resource monitor with pseudographic bars
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]ttbars\f[R] [\f[B]-tmux\f[R]|\f[B]-tty\f[R]|\f[B]-plain\f[R]]
[\f[B]-t\f[R] [\f[I]interval\f[R]]]
[\f[B]-c\f[R]|\f[B]-cm\f[R]|\f[B]-cd\f[R]|\f[B]-cs\f[R]]
[\f[B]-v\f[R]|\f[B]--version\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]ttbars\f[R] is a minimalist system monitor.
It prints key resources as pseudographic bars: CPU, RAM, DISK, ZFS,
Btrfs, and network ↑ TX / ↓ RX.
Use it for a one-shot dump, as a continuous terminal monitor, or as a
\f[B]tmux\f[R](1) status-line plugin.
.PP
\f[B]ZFS\f[R] and \f[B]Btrfs\f[R] bars appear only if pools or volumes
are found.
.PP
The network bar can indicate light traffic in two ways: a more
noticeable label change, or a brief boost of the first scale segment on
small rare packets.
That feature only makes sense with a small
\f[I]measure_interval_sec\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]-t\f[R] [\f[I]interval\f[R]]
Watch mode: redraw in a loop.
Optional \f[I]interval\f[R] (seconds, default 1) sets
\f[I]measure_interval_sec\f[R] and overrides the config.
Example: \f[B]ttbars -t 1\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]-c\f[R], \f[B]-cm\f[R], \f[B]-cd\f[R], \f[B]-cs\f[R]
Color palette demo (\f[I]colour1\f[R]\[en]\f[I]colour255\f[R] and named
colors).
\f[B]-c\f[R] random labels, fixed bar; \f[B]-cm\f[R] compact: numbers /
color names only; \f[B]-cd\f[R] random labels/fill with matched symbol
pairs; \f[B]-cs\f[R] random symbols independently.
.TP
\f[B]-tmux\f[R]
Output for the tmux status line.
Syncs \f[I]measure_interval_sec\f[R] with tmux \f[I]status-interval\f[R]
when available.
.TP
\f[B]-tty\f[R]
Terminal output (ANSI colors).
In tty mode bars wrap by whole groups to the terminal width.
.TP
\f[B]-plain\f[R]
Plain output without tmux/tty markup.
.TP
\f[B]-v\f[R], \f[B]--version\f[R]
Print version and exit.
.PP
With no format flag, the mode is chosen automatically (tmux when
appropriate, otherwise tty).
.SH USAGE
.PP
One-shot dump:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ttbars
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Continuous refresh every second (\f[V]-t\f[R] without a number also uses
1 s):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
ttbars -t 1
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
As a tmux plugin in \f[I]\[ti]/.tmux.conf\f[R] (or TurboTmux
\f[I]\[ti]/.config/ttmux.conf\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
set -g status-left \[aq]#(ttbars -tmux)\[aq]
set -g status-interval 1
set -g status-left-length 100
\f[R]
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION
.PP
Metrics config may be system-wide or per-user.
User config overrides system:
.TP
\f[I]/etc/ttbars.conf\f[R]
All users.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.config/ttbars.conf\f[R]
Personal overrides.
.PP
Example template after install:
\f[I]/usr/local/share/ttmux/ttbars.conf.example\f[R].
You can also set variables at the top of the \f[B]ttbars\f[R] script
instead of using a config file.
.SS Appearance
.PP
Bar symbols:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
S1=\[dq]▪\[dq]          # filled segment       ▪ ▪ ▮ ◉ ▓ • # ▪
S0=\[dq]·\[dq]          # empty segment        · ▫ ▯ ◯ ░ ∘ · -
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Labels, colors, and length \[em] configured via variables
\f[I]id=\[lq]label|color|length\[rq]\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
cpu=\[dq]CPU|colour160|10\[dq]
ram=\[dq]RAM|green|10\[dq]
df=\[dq]|cyan|5\[dq]              # empty label = per-volume names (*_labels or auto); non-empty = group title
zfs=\[dq]|brightblue|5\[dq]
btrfs=\[dq]|brightmagenta|5\[dq]
net_tx=\[dq]↑,▲|yellow|nano\[dq]   # Second label for net_led_label_blink=on
net_rx=\[dq]↓,▼|color151|nano\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Colors are standard colors or index 1-256 (red, green, cyan, brightblue,
214/c214/color214/colour214 etc.).
Color palette demo: \f[B]ttbars -c\f[R] (or \f[B]-cm\f[R] /
\f[B]-cd\f[R] / \f[B]-cs\f[R]).
.PP
Length is a segment count, or \f[I]nano\f[R] \[em] a minimalist one-cell
eight-level scale ⢀⢠⢰⢸⣸⣼⣾⣿.
.PP
df, ZFS and Btrfs: empty appearance label → per-volume text from
\f[I]df_labels\f[R] / \f[I]zfs_labels\f[R] / \f[I]btrfs_labels\f[R] (or
auto names).
Non-empty label (e.g.\ \f[I]DISKS\f[R]) → group title; with empty
**_labels* the bars are bare; if **_labels* is set \[em] title and
per-volume labels together.
.SS DISK
.PP
By default real mounts are shown (block disks, ZFS datasets, NFS, \&...;
no loop/tmpfs).
You can filter the list and set short labels:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
df_usage=\[dq]/,/var\[dq]        # empty = all real mounts except df_exclude; else CSV
df_labels=\[dq]ROOT,DATA\[dq]    # empty = mount names (/, → root)
df_exclude=\[dq]/boot,ds.*\[dq]  # hidden mount points list; simple patterns ok
\f[R]
.fi
.SS ZFS
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
zfs_pools=\[dq]zfs-pool,zfs-backup\[dq]   # empty = all except zfs_exclude
zfs_labels=\[dq]DATA,BACKUP\[dq]
zfs_exclude=\[dq]rpool,test*\[dq]         # hidden pools list
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Btrfs
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
btrfs_mounts=\[dq]/mnt/data,/mnt/backup\[dq]
btrfs_labels=\[dq]DATA,BACKUP\[dq]
btrfs_exclude=\[dq]/boot,ds.*\[dq]        # hidden mount points list
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Network
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
interface=\[dq]\[dq]             # empty = auto-detect
net_max_speed=\[dq]1000\[dq]     # Mbps scale; empty = auto (fallback 100)
net_led_bytes=\[dq]512\[dq]      # light-traffic threshold (bytes per sample)
net_led_bar_boost=on
net_led_label_blink=off
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Polling and cache
.PP
\f[I]measure_interval_sec\f[R] is the window over which stats are
collected and averaged (default 1 s in console).
Override via config or \f[B]-t\f[R].
In plugin mode it syncs with tmux \f[I]status-interval\f[R].
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
measure_interval_sec=1
measure_deadline_ratio_pct=100
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]ttbars\f[R] caches results between runs.
Prefer longer refresh for disks and shorter for fast metrics like CPU:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
cpu_refresh_sec=0.1
ram_refresh_sec=1
df_refresh_sec=30
zfs_refresh_sec=30
btrfs_refresh_sec=30
net_refresh_sec=0.1
\f[R]
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]/etc/ttbars.conf\f[R]
System metrics settings.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.config/ttbars.conf\f[R]
Personal metrics settings (overrides \f[I]/etc\f[R]).
.TP
\f[I]/usr/local/share/ttmux/ttbars.conf.example\f[R]
Example metrics config after install.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.cache/ttbars.state\f[R]
Metrics cache between ticks.
.TP
\f[I]\[ti]/.cache/ttbars.state.lock\f[R]
Lock while updating the cache.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
Empty network bars \[em] check auto-detection with \f[B]grep interface
\[ti]/.cache/ttbars.state\f[R], then set manually if needed:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
interface=\[dq]vmbr0\[dq]
net_max_speed=\[dq]100\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
On some physical terminals, default bar symbols are the most reliable:
\f[I]S1=\[lq]▪\[rq]\f[R], \f[I]S0=\[lq]·\[rq]\f[R].
